This is an announcement for the release of FElt version 1.3.  

FElt is a free system for introductory level finite element analysis.  FElt
is intended largely as a teaching tool.  At this stage it should be able
to handle many of static linear analysis problems that you'd find in a 
one or two term introductory class in finite element analysis.  The
FElt element library currently contains eight elements. 

The system offers three different interface options - a standard command 
line application and two X11 based environments.  Velvet, the primary 
graphical environment allows you to specify your problem in a CAD like way.  
There have been substantial improvements to the velvet GUI which should make 
it a lot easier to use.  We've also added some post-processing to velvet,
including shaded color stress plots for planar problems. Other additions
include a basic Timoshenko beam element, displacement and hinged 
boundary conditions and several fixes/enhancements to the way distributed
loads are handled.  

The User's Guide has tripled in size.  We hope it's actually fairly
useful now.  

Getting FElt ...
FElt has found a permanent home.  The latest version will always
be available from cs.ucsd.edu in /pub/felt.  If you get it from here
you'll probably want to grab a couple of different files.  

felt-1.3.src.tar.Z 		- all source and most documentation for FElt.
				  Get this one if you want to build the
			 	  package yourself (it should be easy).
felt-1.3.hp-pa.tar.Z		- binaries _only_ for HP-UX 9.0 Series 700
				  machines.  
felt-1.3.sparc.tar.Z		- binaries _only_ for Sparc machines.
felt-1.3.linux.tar.Z		- binaries _only_ for Linux.
felt13.zip			- DOS executables for felt and feltvu. 
felt-1.3.ps.Z			- Postscript file for the "FElt User's
			  	  Guide and Reference Manual".  Because
				  of it's size, this is not in the other
				  tar files.  You'll need to get it 
				  separately.
CHANGELOG			- a list of changes from 1.0a -> 1.3
README				- the README file for v1.3
README.alpha			- the README for our initial alpha release.
				  Take a look at these two if you want a
				  a little more information on FElt before
				  you decide to try it.

For the Linux, HP-UX and Sparc binaries you will also need to grab 
felt-1.3.src.tar.Z and felt-1.3.ps.Z.  The source and binary distributions
will untar into the same directory tree so it will look just like you had
made them yourself.  You'll still want to read the INSTALL file to make
sure things end up where the belong. The only reason we don't just pack them
all together is to save space.  For the DOS distribution you'll probably
also still want felt-1.3.ps.Z.

Version 1.3 has also been uploaded to sunsite.unc.edu in 
/pub/Linux/Incoming/felt-1.3.tar.gz.  This file contains all source
and binaries for Linux.  It also includes a copy of the Postscript
file for the User's Guide.  If you don't find it in Incoming it has
probably been moved to /pub/Linux/apps/math

As usual, we would like to stress that feedback is always appreciated ... 
we welcome all comments as to usability and functionality.  If you're using
the package please drop us a note and let us know what you think, even if
you've got no complaints whatsoever.  Mail bug reports, comments, hints,
suggestion, complaints, etc. to one of us at the email addresses below.  

