Here is the README.linux file I included:
---
This is my 'port' of NCSA's Mosaic 2.0 for Linux.  Mosaic is a World
Wide Web browser for X.  See the 'Features' section below for a sample
of Mosaic's capabilities.  This release only supports kernel TCP/IP, a
TERM version will be released later.

I've compiled Mosaic 2.0 using Metrolink's Motif 1.2.2 release for
Linux.  This Mosaic binary is linked statically to the Motif library,
as allowed under Metrolink's license.  You do not need to be a
licensed user of Motif to use this program.


Requirements:
-------------

System: Linux 0.99pl13 or greater.  
Libraries: Libc 4.4.4 w/ ld.so 1.3.  X libraries from XFree86 2.0.
Memory: 8 Mb required.
Disk space: 2 Mb.
External Programs: (recommended) xv 3.0, Ghostscript, mpeg_play,
                   others.  See included documentation.

The XFree86 2.0 server is recommended, but not required.

This program was compiled using gcc's -O6 -m486 -fomit-frame-pointer
options.  This program does not require a 486.

This program was compiled without direct WAIS support, or support for
HDF or DTM.  At some point in the future, I may release Linux binaries
supporting these options.

This release includes the Makefile used for building this binary.  No
other changes were necessary.

The source is available from ftp.ncsa.uiuc.edu, in /Mosaic.  The most
recent Linux binary should be available from sunsite.unc.edu, in
/pub/Linux/system/Network/info-systems.


Features:
---------

Here are a few of Mosaic's features:

 o Support for accessing documents, images, audio, video, animations,
   and data through World Wide Web, Gopher, WAIS, FTP, NNTP/Usenet
   news, telnet, tn3270, and local files; and via gateways, Techinfo,
   TeXinfo, Archie, CSO qi/ph, relational databases, and other
   sources.
 o Friendly X/Motif user interface.
 o Color and monochrome default X resource settings.
 o Multiple independent document viewing windows.
 o Completely interruptible network input/output, with full status
   indication during network operations.
 o Built-in support for recognizing and handling GIF, JPEG, TIFF,
   audio, AIFF, DVI, MPEG, MIME, XWD, RGB, HDF, PostScript files and
   forking off appropriate viewers.
 o Global history with previously visited locations visually distinct;
   global history is persistent across sessions.
 o Hotlist/bookmark capability -- keep list of interesting documents,
   add/remove/rename items, list is persistent across sessions.
 o Personal annotations with GUI annotation entry dialog; annotations
   can later be edited or deleted, and hyperlinks to existing
   annotations are inlined into subsequent accesses of an annotated
   document. (Any document from any server via any access method can
   be annotated.)
 o Transparent and automatic uncompression of compressed (.Z) and
   gzip'd (.z or .gz) files (over FTP, HTTP0, and Gopher).


Credits:
--------

Mosaic was written by Marc Andreessen (marca@ncsa.uiuc.edu) and Eric
Bina (ebina@ncsa.uiuc.edu) at NCSA.  Many thanks.

Send bug reports and complaints to me, Scott Laird,
(lair@midway.uchicago.edu), and thanks to the above people.

