PARASOL - A Simulation Library for Distributed and/or Parallel Systems


PARASOL is  an ANSI C  and C++  compatible library designed  to assist
distributed and/or parallel system developers by providing a simulated
environment on which to test  software designs. It is written entirely
in  ANSI C  for  portability and  runs on  a  variety of  O/S-hardware
platforms.

PARASOL  supports  a  task  modelling  paradigm  where  users  specify
software   by  writing   tasks   which  can   model   reality  to   an
arbitrary  degree   of  precision.  These  tasks   physically  execute
on  a   simulated  distributed   and/or  parallel   environment.  This
environment  is  constructed  through  library  calls  to  the  user's
specification.  Elements of  the  execution  environment include  uni-
and  multi-processor nodes,  point-to-point  communication links,  and
multi-point communication buses.

The  library also  provides a  simulated O/S,  based loosely  on Mach,
which  provides   tools  for  task  management   (including  creation,
migration,   and  destruction),   inter-task  communication   (through
asynchronous ports  and shared  memory), and  synchronization (through
message passing, spinlocks, and semaphores).  Users may use PARASOL to
test  system  designs  through  prototyping and/or  as  a  performance
simulation tool by invoking statistical routines to gather performance
measurements and compute mean behaviours with confidence intervals.

PARASOL can  be ftped from  alfred.ccs.carleton.ca ( 134.117.1.1  ) in
directory pub/parasol as parasol-2.2.tar.Z.

For  more  information   send  an  e-mail  to  Dr.   John  Neilson  at
neilson@scs.carleton.ca or parasol@scs.carleton.ca.

