Greetings,

I have ported my simple, public domain, multi-file/multi-window, binary and
text file editor from MSDOS to Linux.  TDE, the Thomson-Davis editor
version 3.2, is suitable for editing UNIX and DOS text files, DOS binary
files, and not very suitable for UNIX binary files, yet.  TDE auto-senses
CRLF/LF in text files and has a CRLF/LF/BIN toggle.  TDE is a rewrite of
DTE 5.1, Doug Thomson's WordStar-like text editor.  TDE 3.2 features
include:

   Blocks:
      Line, stream, and box blocks.
      Move, delete, copy, kopy, overlay, fill, print, number, and sort.
      Block actions within or between files.

   Files:
      Multiple files limited only by memory.
      Text and binary files.

   Windows:
      Multiple windows limited only by memory.
      Split screen horizontally and vertically
      Changes to text in one window are updated in all windows of same file.
      Interactively resize windows.
      Two windows of same size so two files can be easily compared.

   Other:
      Lines as long a 1040 characters.
      Paren balance.
      Mode/information line.
      Cursor sync.
      Directory list
      Ruler with a cursor pointer
      Pop-up Pull-down command menu


