A company that produces an extremely good X spreadsheet has some
interest in porting it to Linux, if people are interested in buying
it.  In order to help them assess the demand for their product, I am
sending out this very small survey, and I ask that everyone reply who
is interested in seeing a real spreadsheet for Linux.  Filling out
this survey is *NOT* a commitment to buy the product!

Here is an overview of the product, so you know what I am asking
about in the survey:

This is a native X Windows product optimized for Unix platforms -- it
is not derived from any Dos or Windows programs.  It was originally
designed for scientific and engineering research, and is now being
used in all sorts of environments.  For example, stock traders use it
to keep track of the market interactively, scientists and engineers
use it to analyse their incoming data from experiments in real time,
and some people simply use it as a very good normal spreadsheet
program.

Its capabilities include:
 * Real-time data collection and analysis, including real-time
   updating of any graphs you choose.  Graphs may be viewed
   separately or embedded in the spreadsheet.
 * Three-dimensional graphs may be interactively rotated, and
   color is automatically used if available to make the graphs more
   readable.
 * Graphs may be viewed in seperate windows or as elements of the
   spreadsheet.
 * A great many @functions are available for computation.
 * A toolkit (API) is provided which makes customization and even
   extensions to the spreadsheet very easy for anyone familiar with
   C or Fortran. In addition, peer-to-peer connectivity among active
   sheets can also be achieved.
 * Creates its own format of spreadsheet files, which is an ascii
   format, and therefore can be easily emailed and transported between
   different computers with different concepts of binary storage.  It
   also knows the wks and wk formats, and is available on almost any
   platform running X, including most popular versions of UNIX, and
   DECwindows under VMS for good measure.
 * Includes lots of built-in help, and is very easy to learn to use,
   without sacrificing power.
 * The look-and-feel is Motif.

Now, the survey itself:

1) Would you be interested in buying such a spreadsheet?
   [y/n]:
   Why or why not?  What capabilities interest you?  What
   capabilities are you interested in that are not mentioned here,
   but might exist anyway?

2) The current list price is $695, quantity 1.  (Quantity discounts
   are available.)  Would you be willing to pay that price for
   this tool?
   [y/n]:
   If not, what price would you be willing to pay?

3) If a special edition with no real-time capabilities and limited
   features was made available at a lower price, would you be
   interested in purchasing a copy?
   [y/n]:
   If so, what price would you find reasonable?

4) What questions do you have about this product that we could answer?

5) Would you like to be notified if/when this product is made
   available for Linux?

Thanks much for participating in this survey!

michaelkjohnson

