Q: Will future releases of XFree86 support Diamond's Cards?

No.

XFree86 2.0 and greater, will replace XS3-0.4.4 and XS3-928 servers.
Also, the freq, (or s3, or ds3) program that you use to set your
clock, may be incorporated as part of your Xconfig, with the ClockProg
option.

HOWEVER, at the time this FAQ is being written, we are all on the
waiting list for XFree86 2.0.


Q: How do I get my card to work with XFree86, and Linux

Read warning at the top of this document before you attempting any of
these suggestions.

These fixes should work with either XFree86 versions 1.2 & 1.3.

There are a number of fixes, contained in this document.  One may be
just what you were looking for.  (for those with search capability,
search for ``F:'', to find the one for your card.)


F:-------------------- TRY THIS FIRST

Some (if not all) Cirrus logic chipsets do not need any special
program to set the clock frequencies.  If you can get attain the
high-res modes from a *COLD* boot, without running any special clock
programs, or what have you, then you don't need a special fix, like
the rest of us unfortunate Diamond owners.

My understanding, is that that you don't need any special program for
the 25 & 28Mhz Clocks for 640x480 modes.  They're redialy available.
You just have to find the ModeDB timings appropriate for your system.

Also, the high-res modes are accessible without any special program,
you just have to follow steps similiar to these:

Start up DOS, and run a program that places your system in the
high-res video mode which you'd like to operate in.  Then
<ctrl>-<alt>-<delete>, and boot up unix.

At this point the video card clocks are set for that mode.  They may
or may not show up under the clock section, when you startx with the
Clock section commented out.

Do just that.  Place a # symbole in front of the colcks line, in your
Xconfig and then startx > x.log .  This probably won't work, but what
it will do, is report to you, the clocks that XFree sees on you're
video card.

At your'e discression, comment out the ModeDB Lines.  Some systems are
susceptiable to frequencies which surpass the hardware's limit, and
can cause damage to the monitor and/or video card.  

Now replace the clocks line in you're Xconfig with the Values that are
reported in the x.log file.

Now, you have to find the ModeDB settings for the Xconfig.

Clocks 25 28 74
             ^^  the third clock value is the one you want to match
		 your "1024x768" ModeDB section.

ModeDB
#            clock  horizontal timing      vertical timing
 "640x480"   25     640  672  768  800    480  490  492  525
             28     640  672  768  800    480  490  492  525
 "1024x768"  74    1024 1024 1192 1368    768  768  779  806
             ^^ this matches the third clock position under clocks.

In order to pass the proper horizontal & vertical timings for 1024x768
mode the 74 under clocks & clock has to be the same number.  74
doesn't have to be the number but the number has to match in both
sections for XFree to use the correct timings.

Once you have this connected, you have to find the correct horizontal
& vertical timings values, for your card and monitor.  There is a
tutor, that comes with the XFree distribution, for determining the
correct values.

Also, the xclk program works nicely.  I have the source, for xclk in
case you cannot find it.  It is listed under the FAQ, as a binary-only
for Linux.

When you find the correct timings, X should work.

No, every time you shutdown your computer, you'll have to boot up dos,
and set the clocks with windows, or whatever program you find to put
your video card in 1024x768 mode.  -- this, however, can be replaced
by running the freq program, but you have to find correct paramater to
pass to freq.  (ds3 for most Stealths, don't know if ds3 works with
any of SpeedStars).

F:--------------------Joe's Fix:

Hardware: Diamond Stealth 24 (ISA) v1.03
	  486/50 Contaq ISA motherboard
	  NEC MultiSync Plus Monitor

Linux 0.99 pl11  (SLS 1.03 release)

I don't know why xclk is under X-server/s3.  It seems to me that it
should work find, reguardless of the server you use.
(This program is great!!!  Helps computing ModeDB timings)

There's a vgaset which comes with Linux, I think this is the same.
(I used the one that came with Linux SLS 1.03.)

The s3 program in stealth-fix sets the stealth clocks (properly for
1024x768)...  (I guess you have to run the dos stl/util/stlmode
program, to set the clocks, before you'll get any of this to work,
this is purely speculation though.)

With XS3-0.4.4 server linked to /usr/X386/bin/X, and the Clocks line
in Xconfig commented, it reported what the clocks were.  I set the
Clocks line to these values.

I ran the xclk program, with the value of the third clock in the list
reported by startx.

xclk -h 74 1024 >> /usr/X386/lib/Xconfig

After this, I started x, and used vgaset, and found a magical
combination.

This worked for me, though, I do need to rerun vgaset.  My Xterm had a
