Hello there. I'm the author of Seyon, Mumail, Babaya, and Xrz3D. I
developed those programs under linux back when I had a linux system. I
don't anymore, and the programs have not been updated for a few months
now.

I had a lot of fun writing and updating these programs, and they are
in general useful both to the linux community and unix at large.
Unfortunately, it is unlikely that I'd be able to update them any
further, and some of them do need to be updated to remove bugs that
have surfaced recently, to bring them up to date with newer libraries,
or to add more features so that they would be even more useful.

So for them to continue to be useful rather than stagnate, and since
it's unlikely I'd be able to provide that, I'm looking for people to
take them over. As I said, I enjoyed writing and updating these
beasts, and in the process I learned a great deal about programming
for unix, the different subtleties and quirks in every flavor of unix,
and also about writing for the X environment. In addition to that, I
earned a great deal of acquaintance throughout the world -- people who
have used these programs and found them useful, and I'm grateful to
everyone who wrote me or sent a postcard (I have quite a collection of
those now!).

However, since it is unlikely that I can do any further work on them,
I feel it's only fair that someone else takes over so that they
continue to be useful to linux enthusiasts and unix users in general.
I'm therefore looking for people to take over those programs. You can
do anything with them, but I particularly look for people who would
update these programs and enhance them so that they continue to be
helpful to the linux community.

So if you're interested in taking over any of the above programs (or
all of them!), please let me know by email. It would be helpful to
mention what your plans are regarding the program you want, and to
briefly cite your programming background, if any.

Having a puppy program is quite enjoyable. You'll also have a
first-hand experience with writing for the X environment and unix in
general. Besides that, employers always look for someone with
practical experience, and having a program is a good thing to put on
your resume. The most important thing, however, is being enthusiastic
about it. If you don't have that enthusiasm, you'll be doing it for
the wrong reasons, and it wouldn't work for you.

The following is a brief description of each of the program and the
stage it is in right now. If you do take over any of them, I'll try to
answer any questions you may have about the source as much I possible,
and will forward to you all the patches I've got so far.

Seyon: Probably the most well-known. In a nutshell, it's a telecom
program for X11 that I tried to make as easy to use as possible. The
last version released is 2.14b. I have done some maintenanace work on
it since that release and it is now in version 2.20b, which is not
complete and I haven't touched for a few months. This is probably the
most complex of the bunch, but the most useful in terms of learning
about unix.

Mumail: An elm-like mail reader for X11. While it had humble
beginnings, it has grown to a mammoth -- with a multitude of features
that makes it a superset of elm. The last released version is 2.3b,
and I have pretty much done nothing with it since that version. In
terms of coding, this is a better-written program than Seyon (it is a
bit `cleaner').

Babaya: This is a session manager for X11. Not sure if it's
widely-used, but I find most useful. The last released version is
1.1b.  The current one is 1.2b, which is a big update of of 1.1b, and
is essentially complete. This is really a simple program, and wouldn't
demand a lot of experience to master.

Xrz: This is a patch for rz that gives it an X interface. Rather
simple, and it was my first X program. Last released version is 0.4,
which I haven't updated much despite getting a couple of patches to
improve it. I think it needs to be updated to apply cleanly to the
most recent release of rz, but I'm not sure.

Well, sorry for the long-winded article. Please do respond if you want
any of those programs. As I said, you can do as you wish with them,
but the idea is to try make them continue to be useful.

Lastly, I'd like to apologize to everyone who wrote me email in the
past couple of months asking about something related to the programs,
suggesting a change, or just thanking me. I've got about two hundred
such letters in my mail box now, which is more that I can hope to
handle. My apologies for not responding, but I hope you'll understand
my dilemma.

Regards,

